# CCAR-F Gap Question Bank

144 questions total

---

## Part A — Questions on Identified Gaps

### A1. Coordinator re-delegation / iterative refinement loop (Domain 1)

**A1-Q1.** A coordinator's synthesis pass on "causes of the 2008 financial crisis" comes
       back covering only subprime mortgages, omitting credit default swaps and
       ratings-agency failures entirely.
A) Instruct the synthesis agent to "consider more root causes" and re-run synthesis on
   the same findings.
B) Have the coordinator detect the gap against the original scope, then re-delegate
   targeted queries ("credit default swap role," "ratings agency failures") to
   search/analysis before re-running synthesis.
C) Increase the web-search subagent's result count from 10 to 30 sources per query.
D) Add a hook requiring synthesis output to exceed a minimum number of named causes
   before it can return.

**A1-Q2.** A coordinator does re-delegate on detected gaps, but after three rounds of
       re-delegation the report still shows the same one-line gap note every time,
       never actually improving.
A) The re-delegation queries themselves aren't changing round to round — the coordinator
   needs to track what's already been asked and generate genuinely new, narrower
   follow-ups each round.
B) Compaction is destroying the synthesis output between rounds — disable it.
C) The synthesis agent's temperature should be lowered to reduce variance across rounds.
D) Add a fourth mandatory re-delegation round regardless of content, since more
   iterations always converge eventually.

**A1-Q3.** A coordinator notices synthesis is missing coverage of "residential spillover
       effects" and responds by re-invoking the *synthesis* agent again with the
       same source material and the added instruction "please also address
       residential spillover."
A) Correct — re-instructing synthesis with an additional angle is the standard fix for a
   coverage gap.
B) Incorrect — the gap is that the underlying search/analysis never gathered information
   on residential spillover in the first place; re-running synthesis on the same
   incomplete source material can't manufacture facts it was never given.
C) Incorrect — the fix is to lower synthesis's temperature so it extracts more from the
   same sources.
D) Correct, but only if paired with a stricter output length requirement.

**A1-Q4.** A coordinator correctly identifies a coverage gap and re-delegates, but the
       follow-up instruction to the search subagent is identical in wording to the
       original broad research prompt.
A) This is fine — subagents should always receive the same framing to stay consistent
   with the original scope.
B) The re-delegation isn't actually targeted — it should specify the narrow gap itself
   (e.g., "search specifically for residential real estate spillover data") rather than
   repeating the original broad ask, or the search agent will likely surface the same
   results again.
C) The fix is to switch this subagent to a different model tier for the retry.
D) The fix is to have the coordinator itself perform the search directly instead of
   delegating.

### A2. Content vs. metadata separation in subagent handoffs (Domain 1)

**A2-Q1.** A document-analysis subagent hands synthesis a single narrative paragraph
       blending three claims from two different papers, with source names mentioned
       only in passing mid-sentence. The final report cannot reliably attribute
       which claim came from which paper.
A) Have the subagent return each claim as a structured item with its own explicit source
   field, separate from the claim text itself.
B) Ask the subagent to write more clearly so the sources are easier to spot in the
   prose.
C) Have the coordinator manually re-read the original papers to reconstruct attribution.
D) Merge the two papers' findings into one unattributed summary, since fine-grained
   attribution isn't necessary for a synthesis-level report.

**A2-Q2.** Two different source documents are both referred to as "the 2023 report"
       inside a subagent's returned findings, and synthesis conflates them into one
       source in the final citations.
A) Require each finding to carry an explicit, unambiguous document identifier (filename,
   URL, or ID) as its own metadata field, not just a description embedded in prose.
B) Instruct the synthesis agent to try harder to distinguish similarly-named reports.
C) Reduce the number of source documents allowed per subagent to avoid naming
   collisions.
D) Rename all future documents to avoid the word "report" in their titles.

**A2-Q3.** A team's fix for lost citation attribution is to have subagents write
       noticeably longer, more descriptive prose paragraphs around each claim,
       hoping added narrative detail preserves sourcing.
A) Correct — more descriptive prose reliably preserves attribution through downstream
   summarization.
B) Incorrect — attribution loss is a structural problem (content and metadata aren't
   separated into distinct fields); more prose just gives a generic summarizer more
   material to compress and still risks dropping the same inline-mentioned source.
C) Correct, but only for findings under 200 words.
D) Incorrect — the real fix is switching all output to bullet points regardless of
   metadata structure.

**A2-Q4.** A subagent returns page numbers as part of a sentence ("as discussed on page
       12 of the report, revenue grew...") rather than as a separate field, and a
       later compaction pass drops the page reference while keeping the claim.
A) This is acceptable — page numbers are a minor detail not worth structuring
   separately.
B) Give the finding a distinct `page_number` (or general location) metadata field
   alongside the claim text, so it survives independent of whatever happens to the
   surrounding prose during compaction.
C) Avoid ever compacting sessions that reference page numbers.
D) Ask the model to repeat the page number at both the start and end of the sentence for
   redundancy.

### A3. Structured handoff protocols for escalation (Domain 1)

**A3-Q1.** An agent escalates a billing dispute to a human with just the message:
       "Customer needs help, please assist." The human has no transcript access and
       must start the investigation from scratch.
A) This is fine, since humans are trained to investigate cases without prior context.
B) The handoff should include a structured summary: customer/case ID, root cause as
   understood so far, amounts involved, and a recommended next action — so the human can
   act without re-deriving context.
C) The fix is to give the human agent access to the raw conversation transcript instead
   of any summary.
D) The fix is to have the agent resolve the case rather than escalate it, avoiding the
   handoff problem entirely.

**A3-Q2.** A team's escalation handoffs include a full case-facts block copy-pasted from
       the agent's internal working memory, including irrelevant intermediate
       reasoning steps and abandoned lines of investigation.
A) Correct as-is — more context is always better for a human picking up a case.
B) Trim the handoff to the load-bearing structured fields relevant to the case (ID, root
   cause, amount, recommended action) rather than exporting the full internal reasoning
   trace, which buries the actionable summary in noise.
C) Escalation handoffs shouldn't include any case facts at all, to keep them short.
D) Replace the case-facts block with a single-sentence summary and nothing else.

**A3-Q3.** An agent escalates a case but omits a recommended action, reasoning that "the
       human should decide independently, without my bias."
A) Correct — omitting a recommendation avoids improperly influencing the human's
   judgment.
B) Structured handoffs are more effective when they include a recommended action
   alongside the facts — it gives the human a starting point to accept, adjust, or
   reject, without forcing them to reconstruct one from scratch; this doesn't remove
   their judgment, it informs it.
C) The recommendation should only be included if the case involves a refund over $500.
D) The recommendation should replace the root-cause explanation, not accompany it.

**A3-Q4.** A team standardizes escalation handoffs into a fixed structured template
       (case ID, root cause, amount, recommended action) applied to every
       escalation, including ones triggered purely by an explicit customer request
       with no underlying issue investigation done yet.
A) The template should always be fully populated before any escalation is sent, even if
   some fields are empty because no investigation occurred.
B) The template's *purpose* — giving the human enough to act without re-deriving context
   — still applies; fields that reflect work not yet done (e.g., root cause) can
   honestly indicate that, rather than being fabricated to fit the template.
C) Explicit-request escalations shouldn't use structured handoffs at all, since no
   investigation has occurred.
D) The agent should refuse to escalate until it has independently completed a root-cause
   investigation.

### A4. Hooks for data normalization (Domain 1)

**A4-Q1.** A shipping-tracking tool returns delivery timestamps as Unix epoch integers,
       while an inventory tool returns them as ISO 8601 strings. The agent has
       started comparing the raw values directly and drawing wrong conclusions about
       delivery order.
A) Add a system-prompt instruction telling the model to "convert timestamps mentally
   before comparing."
B) Add a `PostToolUse` hook that normalizes both tools' timestamp outputs into one
   consistent format before the model ever reasons over them.
C) Ask both third-party tool vendors to standardize their APIs, and wait for that
   change.
D) Instruct the model to always call a separate "what time is it" tool before comparing
   timestamps.

**A4-Q2.** A team uses a `PostToolUse` normalization hook to reconcile two tools'
       differing status-code conventions (one returns `1`/`0`, the other returns
       `"success"`/`"failure"`), converting both into one consistent boolean before
       the model sees either.
A) This is the wrong use of a hook — normalization should only happen via prompt
   instructions.
B) This is a correct, well-scoped use of a `PostToolUse` hook — reconciling
   heterogeneous but semantically equivalent formats before the model reasons over them,
   distinct from using a hook to *block* an action.
C) This is unnecessary — models can already reason correctly across differing
   status-code conventions without help.
D) This should be an MCP resource instead of a hook.

**A4-Q3.** A tool intermittently returns a business-logic error (e.g., "refund exceeds
       policy limit") formatted as a plain string inside an otherwise
       successful-looking response. The team's fix is a `PostToolUse` hook that
       "normalizes" this by rewriting the string into a friendlier phrase before the
       model sees it.
A) Correct — normalization hooks are the right way to handle any inconsistency in tool
   output, including this one.
B) This misapplies a normalization hook to what's actually a structured-error-metadata
   problem (Domain 2) — the real fix is setting `is_error`/an error category on the
   response, not rewording a business failure to look nicer while still burying it in a
   same-shaped success payload.
C) Correct, but only if the rewritten string is under 15 words.
D) Incorrect — the fix is to remove the hook entirely and have the model parse the raw
   string itself.

**A4-Q4.** A vendor's weather API sometimes returns temperature in Celsius and sometimes
       in Fahrenheit depending on which of its two regional endpoints responds, with
       no indicator in the payload of which unit was used.
A) A `PostToolUse` hook can't help here, since there's no reliable signal in the payload
   to determine which unit to normalize from.
B) A `PostToolUse` hook should default all values to Celsius regardless of source, since
   defaulting is always safer than guessing.
C) The fix is upstream of normalization: this needs a schema/contract fix with the
   vendor (or querying a per-endpoint metadata field, if one exists) — a hook can only
   normalize between formats it can actually distinguish, and this specific failure
   lacks a reliable determinant to normalize *from*.
D) The fix is to have the model always assume Fahrenheit, since that's the more common
   convention for US customers.

### A5. MCP server scoping and configuration (Domain 2)

**A5-Q1.** A developer is experimenting with a new, unreleased internal MCP server that
       only they should use while testing. They configure it in the project's
       `.mcp.json`.
A) This is correct — all MCP servers should be configured at the project level for
   consistency.
B) This should instead be configured in their personal `~/.claude.json`, since it's an
   experimental/personal server not meant to be shared with the rest of the team via
   version control.
C) This should be configured as an environment variable directly, bypassing both config
   files.
D) This should be split across both `.mcp.json` and `~/.claude.json` simultaneously for
   redundancy.

**A5-Q2.** A team's shared Jira MCP server requires an API token. The team wants every
       developer to be able to authenticate with their own individual token without
       anyone's token being committed to version control.
A) Hardcode a single shared token directly in `.mcp.json` and commit it, rotating it
   periodically.
B) Configure `.mcp.json` with `${JIRA_TOKEN}` environment variable expansion, so each
   developer supplies their own token locally while the server config itself stays
   shared and committed.
C) Store each developer's token in their own separate `.mcp.json` file, one per person,
   none of them committed.
D) Avoid using an MCP server for Jira integration entirely, and have developers interact
   with Jira manually.

**A5-Q3.** A project has both a project-scoped `.mcp.json` (with a shared GitHub server)
       and a developer's personal `~/.claude.json` (with an experimental local
       database server) configured at the same time.
A) Only one of the two scopes can be active per session — the developer must choose.
B) Both are discovered and available simultaneously; project and user scope aren't
   mutually exclusive, they're additive.
C) The personal server silently overrides the project server if both define a tool with
   the same name, with no way to detect this.
D) User-scoped servers are automatically disabled whenever a project-scoped `.mcp.json`
   is present.

**A5-Q4.** A new team member joins and complains that a documented internal MCP tool
       isn't available in their session, even though the project's `.mcp.json`
       clearly lists the server.
A) The most likely explanation, given correct project config, is an environment variable
   used in the server's config (e.g., a credential) isn't set in their local
   environment, so the server fails to start for them specifically.
B) `.mcp.json` only applies to the person who created the file, not to teammates who
   clone the repo later.
C) MCP servers require re-registration by every new developer through a separate manual
   process regardless of config files.
D) The tool must have been accidentally scoped to `~/.claude.json` instead, even though
   the file in question is `.mcp.json`.

### A6. MCP resources as content catalogs (Domain 2)

**A6-Q1.** An agent working with a large document-management system currently has to
       call a search tool repeatedly just to figure out what document categories and
       folders even exist before it can decide what to actually look for.
A) Add more search tools with narrower scopes.
B) Expose the folder/category hierarchy as an MCP resource the agent can read directly,
   eliminating the exploratory search calls needed just to discover what's available.
C) Instruct the agent via system prompt to "remember the folder structure between
   sessions."
D) Increase the search tool's result limit so each exploratory call returns more at
   once.

**A6-Q2.** A database-querying agent wastes several tool calls each session just probing
       table names and column structures via trial-and-error `SELECT` queries before
       it can write a real query.
A) Expose the database schema as an MCP resource, giving the agent direct visibility
   into table/column structure without needing exploratory probe queries.
B) Add a hook that blocks any `SELECT` query issued before a specific "discovery" query
   has run first.
C) Have the agent guess table names based on common naming conventions instead of
   probing.
D) Convert the schema into a required parameter that the human must supply manually
   before every query.

**A6-Q3.** A team exposes their document-category catalog as an MCP *tool* called
       `list_categories()` rather than a resource, reasoning "tools and resources
       both return data to the model, so it doesn't matter which one this is."
A) Correct — the choice between tool and resource is purely stylistic for read-only
   catalog data.
B) It works either way, but modeling it as a resource is more appropriate here: this is
   static, non-parameterized, discoverable reference content, exactly what the resource
   abstraction communicates to the model.
C) It should be a hook instead of either a tool or a resource.
D) It should require a `mode` parameter to switch between summary and detailed views,
   which is only possible as a tool.

**A6-Q4.** A team exposes a "generate compliance report" capability as an MCP resource,
       reasoning "it returns data to the model, so resources are the more efficient
       choice here."
A) Correct — any read-facing capability should default to a resource for efficiency.
B) Incorrect — this capability computes a derived result from varying inputs each time
   it runs, which is an action, not static discoverable content; it belongs as a tool
   regardless of the fact that it "returns data."
C) Correct, but only if the report is cached between calls.
D) Incorrect — it should be a hook, since compliance is a policy-enforcement concern.

### A7. Built-in tool selection (Domain 2)

**A7-Q1.** An agent needs to find every file in a large repo whose *filename* ends in
       `.migration.ts`, regardless of what's inside those files.
A) `Grep` for the string ".migration.ts" across all file contents.
B) `Glob` with a pattern like `**/*.migration.ts`, since this is a filename/path pattern
   match, not a content search.
C) `Read` every file in the repo and check its name programmatically.
D) `Bash` with a recursive `cat` across the whole repo, filtering output afterward.

**A7-Q2.** An agent needs to find every place in a codebase that calls a specific
       function, `computeDiscount()`, regardless of which files those calls live in.
A) `Glob` for files matching `**/*discount*`, since the function name suggests likely
   filenames.
B) `Grep` for the string `computeDiscount(` across file contents, since this is a
   content search for a pattern, not a filename search.
C) `Read` the file where `computeDiscount` is defined, and stop there.
D) `Bash` with `find` searching for files modified in the last 30 days.

**A7-Q3.** An agent attempts an `Edit` call to change one specific line in a config
       file, but the target text appears four times identically in the file, and
       `Edit` rejects the call for lack of a unique anchor. The agent's next move is
       to retry the identical `Edit` call two more times, hoping it succeeds
       differently.
A) Correct — retrying is the appropriate response to a non-unique match error.
B) Incorrect — retrying with identical input will fail identically every time; the agent
   should either expand `old_str` to include unique surrounding context, or fall back to
   `Read` the full file and `Write` back the complete corrected content.
C) Correct, but only after first lowering the model's temperature.
D) Incorrect — the fix is to use `Grep` to delete the ambiguous lines instead of editing
   them.

**A7-Q4.** An agent exploring an unfamiliar codebase is asked to "trace how the
       `sendInvoice` function is used throughout the app." It starts by calling
       `Read` on every single file in the repository, one at a time, before deciding
       what's relevant.
A) This is the correct, thorough approach for unfamiliar codebases.
B) A more effective pattern is `Grep` for `sendInvoice` first to locate entry points and
   callers, then `Read` only the specific files that reference it, tracing imports/flow
   from there — rather than reading the entire repo upfront.
C) The agent should use `Glob` for `**/*.ts` first, since that narrows the search to
   TypeScript files.
D) The agent should use `Bash` to count total lines of code in the repo before
   proceeding.

### A8. @import syntax and /memory command (Domain 3)

**A8-Q1.** A large `CLAUDE.md` has grown to include full sections on API conventions,
       testing standards, and deployment procedures, all maintained by different
       team leads who keep conflicting with each other's edits in the same file.
A) `@import` each lead's section from its own separate standards file into the root
   `CLAUDE.md`, keeping the file modular while letting each owner maintain their own
   file independently.
B) Have every lead edit the same file but require a Slack message before making any
   change.
C) Convert each section into a `.claude/commands/` custom command instead.
D) Delete the sections no one has edited recently to shrink the file.

**A8-Q2.** A new team member reports that Claude Code isn't following the team's
       documented testing conventions, even though the conventions are clearly
       written in a `CLAUDE.md` file somewhere in the team's shared setup.
A) Increase the model's context window so it can hold more of `CLAUDE.md` in effective
   attention.
B) Use `/memory` to check which memory files are actually being loaded in their session
   — this directly diagnoses whether the conventions file is in a scope (e.g., a
   teammate's personal `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md`) that never reaches this new member at all.
C) Ask the new team member to manually copy-paste `CLAUDE.md` into every session's first
   message.
D) Rewrite the conventions using more emphatic wording so they're harder to miss.

**A8-Q3.** A root `CLAUDE.md` uses `@import` to pull in
       `./standards/api-conventions.md`, but a developer editing that imported file
       doesn't see their edits reflected in the agent's next session.
A) `@import` only refreshes once per calendar day, so the developer needs to wait.
B) This is likely a path or file-existence issue with the `@import` reference itself, or
   the session needs to reload memory files — `/memory` can be used to verify what
   actually loaded and confirm whether the imported file's content is present.
C) `@import` doesn't support subdirectories, so the file needs to be moved to the root.
D) `@import` is a project-scope-only feature and won't work if the developer is using a
   personal session.

**A8-Q4.** A team wants each package in a monorepo to include only the standards files
       relevant to that package's maintainer's domain (e.g., the backend package
       only pulls in API and database conventions, not frontend styling rules),
       without duplicating shared content across packages.
A) Give every package's `CLAUDE.md` the full set of standards regardless of relevance,
   since more context is always safer.
B) Use `@import` in each package's `CLAUDE.md` to selectively pull in only the standards
   files relevant to that package, keeping shared source files single-sourced while each
   package's effective config stays scoped to what its maintainers actually need.
C) Maintain one single repo-wide `CLAUDE.md` with no `@import` at all, since imports add
   unnecessary indirection.
D) Convert all standards files into skills that must be manually invoked by each
   package's maintainer.

### A9. Path-scoped `.claude/rules/` vs. directory `CLAUDE.md` (Domain 3)

**A9-Q1.** A codebase has GraphQL resolver files scattered across a dozen different
       feature directories (not centralized in one `graphql/` folder), and the team
       wants consistent resolver conventions applied to all of them regardless of
       location.
A) Add the conventions to the root `CLAUDE.md` under a "GraphQL Resolvers" heading and
   trust the agent to recognize when it applies.
B) Create a `.claude/rules/` file with a glob like `paths: ["**/*.resolver.ts"]`, so the
   conventions load automatically whenever a matching file is touched, regardless of
   which of the dozen directories it's in.
C) Create a directory-level `CLAUDE.md` in each of the dozen feature directories,
   duplicating the same conventions in each.
D) Convert the conventions into a skill that must be manually invoked before editing any
   resolver file.

**A9-Q2.** A team has both a directory-level `CLAUDE.md` in `services/payments/`
       (covering that service's specific business rules) and a `.claude/rules/` file
       scoped to `paths: ["**/*.test.*"]` (covering test-file conventions
       repo-wide). A payments test file is being edited.
A) Only one of the two configs can apply at a time — the more specific one wins and the
   other is ignored.
B) Both apply simultaneously and additively: the directory-level
   `services/payments/CLAUDE.md` loads because of location, and the path-scoped
   test-file rule loads because of file-type match — they aren't mutually exclusive
   mechanisms.
C) The path-scoped rule overrides and disables the directory `CLAUDE.md` whenever both
   would apply.
D) Neither applies, since combining directory-scoping and path-scoping on the same file
   isn't supported.

**A9-Q3.** A team wants Terraform infrastructure conventions applied only to `.tf`
       files, which live in one dedicated `infra/` directory with no other file
       types mixed in.
A) A `.claude/rules/` file with `paths: ["**/*.tf"]` is necessary here, since only
   glob-pattern rules can scope by file type.
B) Since `.tf` files here happen to be confined to one single directory with no other
   file types present, a directory-level `CLAUDE.md` in `infra/` would work just as well
   — path-scoped rules earn their advantage specifically when a file type is *scattered*
   across many directories, not when it's already isolated in one.
C) Terraform files require a custom MCP server for convention enforcement; neither
   `CLAUDE.md` mechanism applies.
D) This must use `@import`, since Terraform is an infrastructure concern rather than a
   coding convention.

**A9-Q4.** A `.claude/rules/` file is created with frontmatter `paths:
       ["src/legacy/**"]`, and the team describes this as "using glob-pattern rules
       to keep conventions scoped by file type across scattered directories."
A) This description is accurate for this specific rule.
B) This description is inaccurate for this specific rule: a path like `src/legacy/**`
   scopes by *directory location*, not file type — functionally similar to what a
   directory-level `CLAUDE.md` in `src/legacy/` would achieve, even though it's
   implemented as a `.claude/rules/` file. The glob-pattern advantage over directory
   `CLAUDE.md` specifically shows up for patterns like `**/*.test.ts` that cross many
   directories.
C) This description is accurate, since `.claude/rules/` files are never directory-scoped
   by design.
D) This description is only accurate if the rule also has an `argument-hint`.

### A10. Iterative refinement techniques (Domain 3)

**A10-Q1.** A team asks Claude Code to "reformat all date strings to a standard format"
        across a codebase, but the model produces inconsistent results — some dates
        end up as `MM/DD/YYYY`, others as ISO 8601 — because the prose instruction
        doesn't pin down which standard is meant.
A) Provide 2–3 concrete input/output examples showing exactly which raw formats should
   map to exactly which target format, since concrete examples resolve ambiguity that
   prose alone leaves open to interpretation.
B) Repeat the instruction three times in the prompt for emphasis.
C) Lower the temperature to 0, since the ambiguity is caused by sampling randomness.
D) Split the task across two separate sessions, one for each date format convention.

**A10-Q2.** A developer wants Claude Code to build a caching layer for an unfamiliar
        subsystem, but hasn't yet decided on an invalidation strategy, TTL values,
        or failure-mode behavior, and worries the first implementation attempt will
        bake in unstated wrong assumptions.
A) Have Claude implement its best guess first, then review and correct it afterward.
B) Use the interview pattern: have Claude ask clarifying questions about invalidation
   strategy, TTL, and failure handling *before* implementing, surfacing considerations
   the developer hadn't yet specified.
C) Write the most detailed prose specification possible upfront, covering every edge
   case in exhaustive written detail before any interview questions are asked.
D) Skip planning entirely and rely on Claude Code's default caching conventions.

**A10-Q3.** A team wants a data-migration script to handle null values correctly, but
        plain-language descriptions of "handle nulls gracefully" keep producing
        inconsistent behavior across different tables.
A) Write a test suite with specific test cases showing example input (including null
   values) and expected output, then iterate by sharing test failures to guide
   correction — more effective here than further prose refinement.
B) Add the word "gracefully" in bold in the instruction to increase its weight.
C) Ask the model to handle nulls "the way most migration scripts typically do," relying
   on general convention.
D) Avoid nulls in the source data entirely by preprocessing them out before the
   migration script runs.

**A10-Q4.** A pull request has three separate review comments: a security issue in the
        auth module, a performance issue in an unrelated caching module, and a
        naming-convention nitpick in a third, unrelated file. The developer asks
        Claude Code to address all three.
A) Since these three issues are independent of each other (different modules, no
   interaction), addressing them sequentially, one at a time with separate focused
   messages, is more effective than bundling them into a single dense one; bundling
   multiple issues into one message is better reserved for problems that genuinely
   interact.
B) All three should always be given to Claude in a single combined message regardless of
   whether they interact, since batching is always more token-efficient.
C) Independent issues should be assigned to three different subagents via `Task`, since
   parallel subagent delegation is the correct pattern for any set of more-than-one
   issue.
D) The security issue should be silently deprioritized since it's not related to the
   other two.

### A11. CI/CD integration (Domain 3)

**A11-Q1.** A pipeline script runs `claude "Analyze this diff for security issues"` as
        part of an automated PR check, and the job hangs indefinitely waiting for
        input that will never come.
A) Add `-p` (or `--print`) to run Claude Code in non-interactive mode, which processes
   the prompt and exits without waiting on stdin.
B) Set a generic `CI=true` environment variable, since most CLI tools respect that
   convention automatically.
C) Pipe an empty string into the command via `echo "" | claude ...`, satisfying whatever
   input it's waiting for.
D) Run the command inside a `screen` session so it can be manually attached to and
   dismissed if it hangs.

**A11-Q2.** A CI pipeline needs Claude Code's PR-review findings posted as individual
        inline comments by a separate script, which requires parsing the findings
        into a predictable, machine-readable shape rather than free-form prose.
A) Ask Claude in the prompt to "format the output as JSON" and parse whatever text comes
   back with a regex.
B) Use `--output-format json` together with `--json-schema` to enforce a specific
   structured shape for the output, giving the downstream script a reliable
   machine-parseable result rather than relying on prompt-level formatting requests.
C) Post the entire raw text response as one single PR comment and let developers read
   through it themselves.
D) Run the review twice and diff the two outputs to catch formatting inconsistencies.

**A11-Q3.** A team re-runs an automated code review on a PR after new commits are
        pushed, and the same three issues from the first review pass get posted
        again as duplicate comments on the second pass, annoying the developer.
A) Include the prior review's findings in context on the re-run, and instruct Claude to
   report only new or still-unaddressed issues rather than the full set again.
B) Disable automated re-reviews after the first pass on any PR, checking manually from
   then on.
C) Delete all previous PR comments automatically before every re-run, regardless of
   whether the underlying issues were actually fixed.
D) Lower the review's temperature on re-runs so it becomes more consistent with the
   first pass, including repeating the same findings verbatim.

**A11-Q4.** The same Claude Code session that just wrote a bug fix is then asked, within
        that same session, to review its own diff for regressions before the PR is
        opened.
A) This is the most reliable review setup, since the session already has full context on
   why each change was made.
B) A session reviewing its own just-generated changes retains the reasoning that
   produced them and is less likely to question its own decisions; an independent review
   instance, without that prior reasoning context, is more effective at catching subtle
   issues.
C) This is reliable as long as the session is instructed to "review critically and
   skeptically" before responding.
D) This is reliable as long as extended thinking is enabled for the review step
   specifically.

### A12. Retry-with-error-feedback (Domain 4)

**A12-Q1.** An extraction tool call fails Pydantic validation because a `total_price`
        field came back as the string `"$42.00"` instead of a float. The retry logic
        re-sends the same document with the generic message "please try again."
A) This is sufficient — the model will likely self-correct on a second attempt
   regardless of message content.
B) The retry should include the specific validation error (e.g., "`total_price` must be
   a float; got `'$42.00'` — remove currency symbol") so the model has concrete
   information to correct, rather than guessing what was wrong from a generic prompt.
C) The fix is to lower the extraction tool's temperature to 0 for all retries.
D) The fix is to remove the `total_price` field from the schema entirely, since currency
   formatting is inherently unreliable.

**A12-Q2.** A retry-with-error-feedback loop is set up correctly (specific validation
        errors are fed back on each attempt), but one particular document keeps
        failing after 3 retries because the source document itself never states the
        required field at all — it's genuinely absent, not malformed.
A) Increase the retry cap to 10 for this document, since eventually the model should
   locate the value.
B) Recognize this as a case retry can't fix — the information is absent from the source,
   not incorrectly formatted — and route it to human review or represent the field as
   null, rather than continuing to retry.
C) Rewrite the validation error message to be more detailed, since a clearer error
   message will help the model "find" a value that doesn't exist.
D) Switch to a higher-tier model for this document only, since only a more capable model
   can infer a value that was never stated.

**A12-Q3.** A team implements retry-with-error-feedback, but their retry message
        includes the *previous* failed extraction's incorrect field value without
        stating *why* it was rejected — just "this was wrong, try again."
A) This is sufficient, since restating the failed value alone is enough signal for
   correction.
B) The specific validation error (what rule was violated and why) needs to be included,
   not just a flag that the prior attempt was wrong — without the "why," the model has
   no more information than a blind retry would.
C) The fix is to omit the previous failed value entirely from the retry, showing only
   the original document.
D) The fix is to have a human manually correct the field after the very first failure,
   skipping retries altogether.

**A12-Q4.** A batch of contracts is processed with a retry-with-error-feedback loop. One
        contract fails because its `termination_clause` field is syntactically
        well-typed but semantically nonsensical (an obviously fabricated boilerplate
        string). Validation passes because the schema only checks types, so no retry
        is ever triggered — the bad value ships downstream.
A) This is fine, since schema-level (syntactic) validation and retry logic together are
   sufficient for any extraction pipeline.
B) This is a gap: retry-with-error-feedback only fires on validation *failures*, and
   syntactic validation doesn't catch semantic implausibility — a separate
   semantic/business-rule validation layer is needed to even detect this case before any
   retry logic can act on it.
C) The fix is to increase the retry cap so validation runs more times regardless of
   whether it initially passes.
D) The fix is to remove the `termination_clause` field from the schema, since it's
   clearly unreliable.

### A13. Multi-instance / multi-pass review (Domain 4)

**A13-Q1.** A code-generation session is immediately asked, within the same session, to
        critically review the code it just wrote for edge-case bugs.
A) This is the most efficient setup — the session already understands the code's intent
   and design tradeoffs.
B) The session retains the reasoning that led to its own implementation choices, making
   it less likely to challenge those choices; an independent instance without that
   reasoning context is more likely to catch issues the original session is anchored
   against.
C) This is reliable as long as the review instruction explicitly says "assume this code
   has bugs."
D) This is reliable as long as the review is run a second time within the same session
   for confirmation.

**A13-Q2.** A single review pass across a 22-file refactor produces inconsistent depth
        (some files get thorough analysis, others superficial) and contradicts
        itself — flagging a pattern as risky in one file while approving the
        identical pattern elsewhere in the same PR.
A) Switch to a larger-context-window model so all 22 files get equal attention in one
   pass.
B) Split the review into focused per-file passes for local issues, plus a separate
   cross-file integration pass specifically to catch consistency issues across files —
   addressing both attention dilution and the self-contradiction problem directly.
C) Require the developer to split the PR into smaller PRs before any review runs.
D) Run the single-pass review three times and average the findings.

**A13-Q3.** A team runs a second, independent review instance on generated code
        (correctly avoiding self-review), but that second instance is given the
        exact same system prompt, same file order, and no additional context beyond
        what the first instance had.
A) This still meaningfully addresses the self-review problem — the key variable is the
   absence of the *first instance's own reasoning trace*, not necessarily variation in
   every other input; a fresh instance without that reasoning context is still
   positioned to catch what the generator was anchored against.
B) This provides no benefit at all, since identical prompts and file order guarantee
   identical results regardless of prior reasoning context.
C) This is invalid unless the second instance uses a different model tier than the
   first.
D) This is invalid unless extended thinking is enabled specifically for the second pass.

**A13-Q4.** A team wants to review a monolithic 40-file migration PR for both per-file
        correctness and cross-file consistency, but budget constraints mean only one
        review pass is affordable.
A) A single pass covering all 40 files at once is the correct tradeoff, since splitting
   into multiple passes always costs more regardless of PR size.
B) If only one pass is affordable, the team should recognize the coverage tradeoff being
   made — a single pass risks both the attention-dilution and cross-file-inconsistency
   failure modes that the two-pass split (per-file + integration) exists specifically to
   avoid; this is a real constraint, not a case where a single pass is architecturally
   equivalent to two.
C) The fix is to skip file-level review entirely and only run the integration pass.
D) The fix is to have the developer manually review half the files and Claude review the
   other half.

### A14. Escalation triggers (Domain 5)

**A14-Q1.** A customer explicitly writes, "Please connect me to a human representative,"
        in a case that the agent could likely resolve autonomously in one tool call.
A) The agent should attempt autonomous resolution first, since it's within its
   demonstrated capability, and only escalate if the customer explicitly reiterates the
   request afterward.
B) The explicit request for a human is itself a valid, standalone escalation trigger,
   independent of the agent's own assessment of case complexity — it should be honored
   without first attempting to talk the customer out of it via an unrequested resolution
   offer.
C) The agent should ask the customer to first confirm they've tried self-service
   resources before escalating.
D) The agent should escalate only if the case would take more than two tool calls to
   resolve.

**A14-Q2.** An agent encounters a request for a price match against a competitor, and
        company policy explicitly addresses price matching only for the company's
        own past sales, saying nothing about competitor pricing.
A) The agent should apply the closest analogous policy (its own-sale price matching
   rule) directly to the competitor case, since some policy exists on the general topic.
B) This is a policy gap, not a policy violation — the case should be escalated, since
   the agent has no actual authoritative guidance covering this specific scenario,
   rather than extrapolating a rule that was never written to cover it.
C) The agent should decline the request outright, since no explicit policy authorizes
   it.
D) The agent should resolve it based on general reasonableness, since escalation should
   be reserved only for angry customers.

**A14-Q3.** An agent uses a self-reported confidence score (e.g., "I am 90% confident
        this is correct") as its sole criterion for whether to escalate a case.
A) This is a reliable escalation mechanism, since the model has direct introspective
   access to its own uncertainty.
B) Self-reported confidence is a weak proxy for actual case complexity — a model can be
   confidently wrong on genuinely hard cases — so this shouldn't be the sole escalation
   criterion; explicit criteria and few-shot examples of when to escalate are more
   reliable.
C) This is reliable only when combined with a sentiment-analysis score on the customer's
   message.
D) This is reliable as long as the confidence threshold is set above 95%.

**A14-Q4.** A customer's message shows clear frustration ("this is the third time I've
        had to explain this!") but never explicitly asks for a human, and the
        underlying issue is one the agent can resolve immediately.
A) Escalate immediately without attempting resolution, since expressed frustration alone
   should always trigger escalation regardless of the agent's capability.
B) Acknowledge the frustration and offer resolution given the issue is within the
   agent's demonstrated capability, escalating only if the customer subsequently
   reiterates a preference for a human — distinct from the explicit-request case
   (A14-Q1), where the request itself is the trigger.
C) Ignore the expressed frustration entirely and proceed straight to resolution without
   acknowledgment.
D) Use a sentiment score threshold to decide whether frustration is "high enough" to
   warrant escalation.

### A15. Crash recovery via structured state manifests (Domain 5)

**A15-Q1.** A multi-agent pipeline processing a large research task crashes mid-run
        after several subagents have already completed and returned findings. The
        team wants to resume without re-running the already-completed subagents.
A) Restart the entire pipeline from scratch, since partial state can't reliably be
   trusted after a crash.
B) Have each agent export its state (completed status, findings) to a known location as
   it finishes, and have the coordinator load a manifest of that state on resume,
   re-injecting completed findings into its context rather than re-running finished
   work.
C) Rely on the conversation history alone to reconstruct what had completed, without any
   separate exported state.
D) Increase the context window so the entire session, including the crash point, fits in
   a single request on resume.

**A15-Q2.** A coordinator resumes after a crash and reloads a manifest showing subagent
        A and B completed, but subagent C was mid-execution when the crash occurred.
        The coordinator treats C's absence from the manifest as "not started" and
        simply re-invokes it from the beginning.
A) Correct — since C's manifest entry is absent, restarting it cleanly from scratch is
   the appropriate and safe default.
B) Incorrect — this conflates "not started" with "started but interrupted"; without
   distinguishing these, the coordinator risks either safely redoing C's work
   (acceptable if C is idempotent) or, if C had side effects mid-execution, redoing
   something that shouldn't be repeated — the manifest design needs to capture
   in-progress state, not just complete/absent.
C) Correct, and this generalizes to never needing manifests for any subagent whose work
   might be re-runnable.
D) Incorrect — the fix is to prevent any crash from ever occurring
   mid-subagent-execution.

**A15-Q3.** A team implements crash recovery purely by resuming the raw conversation
        history (`--resume`) for a multi-agent pipeline, with no separate structured
        state export from each agent.
A) This is equivalent to structured state manifests, since conversation history
   technically contains everything that happened.
B) This works for a single continuous interactive session, but for a multi-agent
   pipeline with several independently-executing subagents, structured per-agent state
   exports (a manifest) are the more reliable resumption path — raw history doesn't
   cleanly capture which discrete units of work completed vs. were interrupted
   mid-flight across separate agent contexts.
C) This is strictly better than manifests, since it requires no additional export logic.
D) This only fails if the crash occurred during compaction specifically.

**A15-Q4.** A coordinator's manifest-based resumption correctly reloads completed
        subagent findings, but the findings themselves are just raw text blobs with
        no indication of which stage of the pipeline produced them or what inputs
        they were derived from.
A) This is sufficient, since the coordinator can infer the stage from context.
B) The manifest and its referenced findings should include enough structured metadata
   (stage, inputs used) for the coordinator to correctly re-inject them into the right
   point in the resumed pipeline — a raw blob without that context risks being
   misapplied or re-processed unnecessarily.
C) This is sufficient as long as findings are sorted alphabetically.
D) The fix is to avoid manifests entirely and re-run the whole pipeline whenever
   ambiguity like this exists.

### A16. Provenance and multi-source synthesis (Domain 5)

**A16-Q1.** Two credible sources report different figures for the same statistic (one
        says a market grew 12%, another says 18%) in the same year. The synthesis
        agent picks 18% because it "seemed like a more complete, recent-sounding
        source" and reports only that number as fact.
A) This is correct handling, since choosing the more authoritative-seeming source is the
   synthesis agent's job.
B) Both figures should be preserved with source attribution and explicitly flagged as a
   contested/conflicting data point, rather than the synthesis agent silently resolving
   the conflict by picking one.
C) The lower figure should always be reported, since it's the more conservative
   estimate.
D) Neither figure should be reported, since any conflict between sources makes the
   statistic unusable.

**A16-Q2.** A subagent's finding states a company's revenue figure without noting when
        that figure was published or collected. Later, synthesis flags an apparent
        contradiction with a more recent figure from another source, treating it as
        an inconsistency requiring investigation.
A) This is correctly flagged as a genuine data-quality problem requiring investigation.
B) Requiring publication/collection dates in structured findings would likely resolve
   this — the "contradiction" may simply be a temporal difference (the company's revenue
   changed over time) rather than a genuine inconsistency, and without dates this can't
   be distinguished.
C) The fix is to always use the most recently retrieved finding and discard older ones
   automatically.
D) The fix is to remove numerical figures from synthesis output entirely to avoid this
   class of conflict.

**A16-Q3.** A synthesis agent compresses several subagents' findings into a final
        report, and in the process, claim-to-source mappings that existed in the
        original findings (which claim came from which document) are lost — the
        final report states facts with no attribution at all.
A) This is acceptable, since a synthesis-level report doesn't need to preserve
   source-level granularity.
B) Synthesis should be required to preserve and merge the structured claim-source
   mappings it receives, carrying attribution through to the final output rather than
   compressing it away — losing it during synthesis is exactly the failure this task
   statement addresses.
C) The fix is to have the coordinator re-derive attribution after the fact by re-reading
   all original source documents.
D) The fix is to only report claims that came from a single source, discarding any claim
   confirmed by multiple sources.

**A16-Q4.** A report combines financial figures, recent news, and technical methodology
        findings, and the team's synthesis agent renders all three types of content
        in the same uniform bullet-point-list format for consistency.
A) This is the correct approach, since uniform formatting is always clearer than mixed
   formatting.
B) Different content types are better served by different renderings — financial data as
   tables, news as prose, technical findings as structured lists — rather than
   flattening everything into one uniform format, which can obscure the kind of
   information each section actually represents.
C) This is correct as long as every bullet point includes a citation.
D) This is correct only for reports under 1000 words.

