### B15. MCP tool description writing — distinguishing semantically similar tools

**B15-Q1.** Two MCP tools, `fetch_record` and `get_record`, both described only as
        "Retrieves a record." They differ in that `fetch_record` retrieves from a
        live external API (slower, rate-limited) while `get_record` retrieves from a
        local cache (fast, may be slightly stale). The agent frequently picks
        whichever tool name it encounters first, regardless of which behavior is
        actually appropriate.
A) Rename `get_record` to `fetch_record_v2` to signal it's the newer,
   generally-preferred option.
B) Rewrite both descriptions to explicitly state the behavioral difference (live
   external API vs. local cache; speed/freshness tradeoff) so the model can choose based
   on the actual requirement (freshness-critical vs. speed-critical) rather than
   arbitrary ordering.
C) Remove `get_record` entirely, since having two similarly-purposed tools is inherently
   a problem regardless of their actual behavioral difference.
D) Add a `mode` parameter to `fetch_record` that internally decides whether to hit the
   cache or the live API, without changing either tool's description.

**B15-Q2.** An MCP server exposes `summarize_document` and `extract_key_points`, both
        with detailed, well-written but *functionally overlapping* descriptions —
        both mention "producing a condensed version of the document's content" as
        their core purpose, with no clear differentiator.
A) The descriptions being long and detailed is sufficient regardless of whether they
   actually distinguish the two tools' purposes from each other.
B) Length and detail aren't the same as differentiation — the descriptions need to state
   *how* the two outputs actually differ (e.g., prose narrative summary vs. structured
   bulleted key-point extraction) so the model can select correctly between genuinely
   similar-sounding tools, not just be told more about each in isolation.
C) The fix is to merge both into one tool called `condense_document`, since
   consolidation always resolves description overlap.
D) The fix is to add more few-shot examples to the system prompt rather than touching
   either tool's description.

**B15-Q3.** A team notices their agent frequently calls `analyze_transaction` when the
