        string.
A) This is an acceptable design as long as the combined description is sufficiently long
   and detailed.
B) Splitting into purpose-specific tools (`summarize_document`, `extract_data_points`,
   `verify_claim_against_source`), each with its own focused description and
   input/output contract, would reduce the selection ambiguity inherent in one
   overloaded tool with a mode parameter buried in prose.
C) This is acceptable as long as `tool_choice` is set to `"any"` to force a call
   regardless of mode confusion.
D) This is acceptable as long as the tool is renamed to something more general, like
   `handle_document`.

### B16. MCP tools and resources — reducing exploratory tool calls

**B16-Q1.** An agent working with a support-ticket system has to call a
        `list_ticket_categories` tool at the start of nearly every session just to
        learn what categories exist, before it can meaningfully categorize anything.
A) Leave this as a tool, since any data the model needs to retrieve should be modeled as
   a tool by default.
B) Expose the category list as an MCP resource instead — since it's static, discoverable
   reference content rather than a computed action, this eliminates the near-universal
   exploratory call at the start of every session.
C) Have the agent memorize the category list once and never re-check it, even across
   unrelated sessions.
D) Add a `PreToolUse` hook that blocks any ticket-categorization action until
   `list_ticket_categories` has been called at least once, formalizing the exploratory
   call rather than eliminating it.

**B16-Q2.** A codebase-exploration agent repeatedly calls a `list_project_structure`
        tool at the start of many unrelated tasks within the same session, since the
        project's file/module layout doesn't change during the session but isn't
        persisted anywhere the agent can check without calling the tool again.
A) This is fine and expected — re-deriving structure at the start of every task is the
   correct default regardless of whether it changed.
B) Since this is static content for the duration of the session, exposing it as an MCP
   resource (or otherwise making it available without a fresh tool call each time) would
   eliminate the repeated exploratory calls for information that hasn't changed.
C) The fix is to increase the tool's rate limit so repeated calls are cheaper.
D) The fix is to convert `list_project_structure` into a required parameter on every
   other tool, forcing it to always be supplied.

**B16-Q3.** A database-driven agent is given both a `run_query` tool (for arbitrary SQL)
        and, separately, the full database schema exposed as an MCP resource.
        Despite the resource being available, the agent still issues several
        `run_query` calls just to probe table and column names before writing its
        actual query.
A) This suggests the resource isn't actually being surfaced to or read by the agent
   effectively — the fix is to verify the resource is genuinely being made visible/read
   (e.g., via description or explicit reference) so the agent uses it instead of falling
   back to exploratory queries it already has an alternative to.
B) This is expected and cannot be improved — probing via `run_query` is inherently
   necessary regardless of what resources are available.
C) The fix is to remove `run_query` entirely, since resources should always replace
   tools for any data-related need.
D) The fix is to lower the model's temperature so it stops probing and commits to a
   query.

**B16-Q4.** A team debates whether a frequently-changing (but read-only,
        side-effect-free) live inventory count should be modeled as an MCP resource
        or a tool, given that resources are typically associated with static
        content.
A) It must be a tool, since resources can only represent unchanging content by
   definition.
B) It can still be modeled as a resource — the determining property is
   read-only/no-side-effects, not staleness or change-frequency; a resource can
   represent live, frequently-changing data as long as reading it doesn't compute a
   derived value from varying inputs or cause side effects.
C) It must be split into two separate capabilities, one resource for old counts and one
   tool for current counts.
D) It must be modeled as a hook, since hooks are the correct mechanism for anything that
   changes frequently.

### B17. Specialized review passes separating concerns (security / business logic / API
design)

**B17-Q1.** A single review pass over a PR is asked to check for security issues,
        business-logic correctness, and API design consistency all at once, using
        one generic set of few-shot examples covering all three loosely. The team
        notices security issues get caught reliably, but business-logic issues are
        frequently missed.
A) This is expected and acceptable, since no review process can catch every issue
   category equally well.
B) Splitting into separate, concern-specific passes — each with its own focused few-shot
   examples for that concern (security examples for the security pass, business-logic
   examples for the business-logic pass) — would likely improve recall on the
   currently-underserved category without necessarily sacrificing the security pass's
   existing strength.
C) The fix is to remove the security-focused few-shot examples, since they seem to be
   crowding out the other concerns.
D) The fix is to run the same generic pass three times and average results across runs.

**B17-Q2.** A team splits reviews into three concern-specific passes (security, business
        logic, API design), but gives all three passes the exact same generic
        few-shot examples, just relabeling which pass they belong to.
A) This fully realizes the benefit of concern-based splitting, since the passes are now
   structurally separate.
B) Structural separation alone doesn't deliver the benefit without concern-specific
   examples — each pass needs few-shot examples actually representative of *that*
   concern's characteristic issues; reusing the same generic examples across all three
   passes undercuts the reason for splitting in the first place.
C) This is acceptable as long as each pass uses a different model tier.
D) This is acceptable as long as each pass is run in a separate session.

**B17-Q3.** A team observes that their security-focused review pass, when combined into
        a single pass with business-logic review, tends to miss subtle security
        issues in favor of flagging more business-logic issues, and vice versa when
        the emphasis shifts — a recall tradeoff between the two concerns within one
        combined pass.
A) This is unrelated to concern separation and is purely a model-capability limitation
   with no architectural fix.
B) This is exactly the kind of recall tradeoff separating concerns into focused passes
   is meant to prevent — each pass, focused on one concern with matching few-shot
   examples, isn't competing for attention or recall against a different concern's issue
   patterns within the same pass.
C) The fix is to increase the reviewing model's context window, which directly addresses
   recall tradeoffs between concerns.
D) The fix is to alternate which concern is emphasized on each PR, rotating focus rather
   than running dedicated passes.

**B17-Q4.** A team implements three concern-specific review passes, but runs them in a
        way where the business-logic pass's few-shot examples inadvertently include
        a security-relevant example (mislabeled), effectively re-introducing some of
        the cross-concern confusion the split was meant to avoid.
A) This is a minor, inconsequential detail, since the pass will still generally focus on
   business logic regardless of one mislabeled example.
B) This partially undermines the purpose of the split — few-shot examples are a primary
   signal for what a pass should attend to, so a mislabeled example bleeding a different
   concern back into a supposedly focused pass reintroduces some of the same
   cross-concern interference the separation was designed to prevent.
C) This is fine as long as the security pass doesn't have a similarly mislabeled
   example.
D) This is fine as long as the passes are still run in three separate API calls,
   regardless of example content.

### B18. JSON schema and `tool_choice` to enforce structured compliance

**B18-Q1.** A structured-extraction pipeline needs the model to *always* return a
        `tool_use` block, never plain text, for every document — including unusually
        formatted ones the pipeline hasn't seen before.
A) Set `tool_choice` to `"auto"`, since this gives the model maximum flexibility to
   decide the best response format per document.
B) Force the specific extraction tool via `tool_choice: {"type": "tool", "name":
   "extract_fields"}`, guaranteeing a `tool_use` block every time, while designing the
   schema itself (nullable fields, an "unclear"/"other" pattern) to honestly represent
   hard-to-parse cases instead of relying on the model escaping into prose.
C) Set `tool_choice` to `"any"`, since this is functionally identical to forcing a
   specific named tool.
D) Leave `tool_choice` unset and add a system-prompt instruction insisting the tool
   always be used.

**B18-Q2.** A pipeline has three different extraction schemas depending on document
        type, and the document type isn't known in advance. The team wants to
        guarantee the model calls *some* extraction tool (matching whichever schema
        fits), without pre-forcing one specific tool by name.
A) `tool_choice: "auto"`, since "auto" is the most flexible general-purpose setting.
B) `tool_choice: "any"` — this guarantees a tool call happens (unlike "auto," which
   permits plain text), while still letting the model choose which of the three schemas
   actually fits the document, unlike forcing one specific named tool upfront.
C) Force a single combined tool covering all three document types' fields
   simultaneously, since consolidating removes the need for `tool_choice` configuration
   at all.
D) Leave `tool_choice` unset, since schema selection should always be left entirely to
   system-prompt instruction.

**B18-Q3.** A JSON schema for an invoice-extraction tool passes strict validation
        (correct types, all required fields present) on every extraction, yet a
        downstream process discovers that several "valid" extractions have negative
        `total_amount` values or line items that don't sum to the stated total.
A) This proves the JSON schema approach doesn't actually guarantee anything and should
   be abandoned.
B) Strict schema validation via tool use eliminates *syntax* errors (wrong types,
   missing fields) but doesn't prevent *semantic* errors like implausible or
   internally-inconsistent values — a separate semantic/business-rule validation layer
   is needed for that, which is a distinct guarantee from what `tool_use` + JSON schema
   alone provides.
C) The fix is to switch `tool_choice` from "auto" to "any," which will also catch
   semantic errors.
D) The fix is to add more required fields to the schema, since semantic errors are cured
   by making more fields mandatory.

**B18-Q4.** A team wants to guarantee that a specific tool, `extract_metadata`, always
        runs *before* any of several possible enrichment tools, but leaves the
        choice of which enrichment tool to run afterward up to the model based on
        the metadata result.
A) Set `tool_choice` to force `extract_metadata` specifically on the first turn, then
   process the following turn(s) with `tool_choice` set to `"auto"` (or `"any"`) so the
   model can choose the appropriate enrichment tool based on what metadata was found.
B) Set `tool_choice` to force `extract_metadata` for every turn of the entire
   conversation, including all enrichment steps.
C) This can't be achieved with `tool_choice` at all; it requires a `PreToolUse` hook to
   sequence the calls instead.
D) Set `tool_choice` to `"any"` for the entire conversation, since "any" already
   guarantees a specific ordering by default.

### B19. Output truncation on large review tasks → splitting into smaller-scoped API
tasks

**B19-Q1.** A code-review request covering a 60-file PR in a single API call produces a
        response that gets cut off mid-review, with `stop_reason: "max_tokens"`,
        leaving the review incomplete for the later files.
A) Simply increase `max_tokens` indefinitely for every review request, regardless of PR
   size, as a universal fix.
B) Split the review into multiple smaller-scoped API calls (e.g., batches of files, or
   one call per file) so each individual request's expected output comfortably fits
   within reasonable output limits, rather than relying on one call to cover an
   arbitrarily large PR.
C) Reduce the review's thoroughness instructions so the output is naturally shorter,
   regardless of PR size.
D) Ignore the truncation, since a partial review of the first files is still better than
   no review at all.

**B19-Q2.** A team increases `max_tokens` substantially to avoid truncation on large
        reviews, and truncation stops occurring — but reviews of very large PRs
        still show the same attention-dilution symptoms as before (inconsistent
        depth across files, contradictory findings).
A) This proves `max_tokens` was never the real issue for output quality — increasing it
   fixed the truncation failure specifically, but attention dilution across a very large
   single-pass review is a separate problem (addressed by splitting into
   per-file/integration passes), not one `max_tokens` alone resolves.
B) This proves the model has a fundamental capability limitation that no architectural
   change can address.
C) The fix is to increase `max_tokens` even further until the dilution symptoms also
   disappear.
D) The fix is to lower the temperature, since dilution is caused by generation
   randomness.

**B19-Q3.** A team splits a large review into smaller-scoped API calls (one per file, to
        avoid truncation) but each call is given zero context about the other files,
        causing the review to miss a cross-file bug where two files' changes
        conflict with each other.
A) This is an unavoidable and acceptable cost of avoiding truncation — cross-file issues
   simply can't be caught once you split into smaller calls.
B) Splitting into smaller-scoped calls to solve truncation is correct, but a *separate*
   cross-file integration pass (with visibility across the split files) is still needed
   to catch issues like this — solving truncation via splitting doesn't automatically
   solve the cross-file-visibility need, which requires its own dedicated pass.
C) The fix is to revert to one giant single call despite the truncation risk, since
   that's the only way to preserve cross-file visibility.
D) The fix is to randomly assign files to review calls instead of splitting by logical
   grouping, which would somehow catch more cross-file issues.

**B19-Q4.** A team splitting a large review into smaller API calls chooses to split
        strictly by file *count* (e.g., "10 files per call") rather than by any
        logical grouping, resulting in some calls covering files from entirely
        unrelated modules while others happen to split a tightly-coupled pair of
        files across two different calls.
A) This is the correct approach, since splitting only needs to satisfy an output-size
   constraint, and grouping logic is irrelevant.
B) A more effective split would group files with logical relationships (e.g., files that
   import each other, or belong to the same module) into the same call where possible,
   since splitting purely by count risks the exact cross-file-visibility problem
   (B19-Q3) within calls that arbitrarily separate related files.
C) This is the correct approach as long as the file count per call is exactly 10, since
   that number is a documented best practice regardless of file relationships.
D) This is the correct approach as long as a single additional integration pass is
   skipped entirely, since count-based splitting is assumed to already handle
   relationships.

### B20. Reducing false-positive rates via project-specific conventions and accepted
patterns

**B20-Q1.** An automated review agent repeatedly flags a specific coding pattern as a
        bug, but the pattern is actually an intentional, well-known convention
        specific to this team's codebase (e.g., a deliberately unusual null-check
        idiom used throughout for a documented reason).
A) Tell the reviewer to "be more conservative" and "only report high-confidence
   findings" in the system prompt.
B) Supply the specific accepted pattern directly to the reviewer (e.g., in `CLAUDE.md`
   or few-shot examples) as a known, intentional convention, so the reviewer can
   recognize and stop flagging this specific pattern rather than relying on vague
   conservatism instructions that don't target the actual false-positive source.
C) Lower the reviewer's temperature to 0, since false positives are caused by sampling
   variance in pattern recognition.
D) Remove the review category responsible for null-check-related findings entirely,
   losing legitimate detections along with the false positive.

**B20-Q2.** A team's fix for a high false-positive rate on a specific finding category
        is to instruct the reviewer to "only report issues you're highly confident
        about" for that category, without supplying any actual examples of what
        should or shouldn't be flagged.
A) This reliably reduces false positives to an acceptable level, since confidence-based
   filtering directly targets the problem.
B) General confidence-based instructions like this tend not to improve precision
   compared to supplying explicit, concrete criteria or accepted-pattern examples — the
   model's confidence isn't necessarily calibrated to whether a specific pattern is
   actually a team-accepted convention, which is what's actually needed here.
C) This works reliably, but only if paired with a corresponding decrease in
   `max_tokens`.
D) This works reliably as long as the instruction is repeated at both the start and end
   of the system prompt.

**B20-Q3.** A team wants to reduce false positives on a review category without losing
        the category's genuine detection value entirely, given that developers have
        started ignoring the category's output due to accumulated distrust.
A) Permanently remove the entire finding category, since developer trust, once lost,
   cannot be recovered for that category.
B) A reasonable interim approach is to temporarily disable the high-false-positive
   category while supplying concrete accepted-pattern examples and refining its
   criteria, then re-enable it once precision improves — restoring trust without
   permanently sacrificing the category's genuine value.
C) Increase the review frequency for that category, since running it more often will
   naturally improve its precision over time.
D) Merge the problematic category into an unrelated, better-performing category so its
   false positives are diluted by the other category's accuracy.

**B20-Q4.** A team supplies a large set of accepted-pattern examples to reduce false
        positives, but all the examples happen to come from just one part of the
        codebase (the API layer), while the false-positive problem is actually
        occurring across multiple different subsystems with their own distinct
        accepted conventions.
A) This is sufficient, since accepted patterns from any one part of the codebase
   generalize automatically to every other part.
B) The examples need to be representative of where the false positives are actually
   occurring — accepted patterns are often subsystem-specific, so examples drawn from
   only the API layer won't necessarily transfer to reducing false positives in, say,
   the database layer's own distinct conventions.
C) The fix is to increase the number of API-layer examples further, since more examples
   from the same source will eventually generalize regardless of subsystem.
D) The fix is to remove all examples and rely solely on the model's default judgment
   across all subsystems.

